  "ch1.title": "Money is an IOU everyone accepts",
  "ch1.law": "Money is not a thing. It is an IOU. Whichever IOU everyone is willing to accept becomes money.",
  "ch1.p1": "Imagine a small town with no money. The baker wants fish, the fisherman wants shoes, and the cobbler wants bread. Each of the three has something the others want, yet no two of them can trade. The fisherman doesn't need bread, and the cobbler doesn't need fish. To trade by swapping goods directly, you have to find someone who <strong>happens to have what you want and happens to want what you have</strong>.",
  "ch1.p2": "The fix is to keep a record. The baker takes the fish and writes the fisherman a note: “I owe you one loaf of bread.” The fisherman takes the note to the cobbler and buys shoes with it. The cobbler then takes the note to the baker and collects his bread. The accounts balance, and all three get what they wanted. This only works if everyone who receives a note trusts the person who wrote it.",
  "ch1.lab.title": "48 people, 8 kinds of goods, 3 ways to pay",
  "ch1.lab.how": "Each person makes one kind of good and wants another. Every round, everyone is paired off at random. The three rows of dots are the same group meeting in the same order; only the way they pay differs. A lit dot means that person has got the good they wanted. Drag the number of goods and the trust in private IOUs, and watch the three curves separate.",
  "ch1.cv": "Share of people who have got the goods they need, by round, under three ways of paying",
  "ch1.ctl.n": "Kinds of goods",
  "ch1.ctl.q": "Trust in private IOUs",
  "ch1.btn.restart": "Restart",
  "ch1.btn.step": "Run 10 more rounds",
  "ch1.out.n": "{n} kinds",
  "ch1.out.q": "{q}%",
  "ch1.row.barter": "Barter",
  "ch1.row.personal": "Private IOUs",
  "ch1.row.bank": "Bank IOUs",
  "ch1.ax.x": "Round →",
  "ch1.ax.y": "↑ People who have traded",
  "ch1.r.round": "Rounds run",
  "ch1.r.barter": "Barter: traded",
  "ch1.r.personal": "Private IOUs: traded",
  "ch1.r.bank": "Bank IOUs: traded",
  "ch1.r.unit.round": "{v} rounds",
  "ch1.r.unit.pct": "{v}%",
  "ch1.say.n2": "With only two kinds of goods, meeting the other type always leads to a trade: what you want is exactly what they have, and what they want is exactly what you have. IOUs don't add much here.",
  "ch1.say.main": "With {n} kinds of goods, <em>{stuck}</em> people can never trade under barter: nobody in town has both what they want and wants what they make. Holding a bank IOU, they can buy what they need after an average of <em>{eb}</em> rounds. Private IOUs are accepted by only {q}% of people, and it takes an average of <em>{ep}</em> rounds.",
  "ch1.p3": "The problem with barter has a name: the <strong>double coincidence of wants</strong>. Every trade needs two desires to match at the same moment. The more kinds of goods there are, the fewer chances there are of a match, and the faster they fall off. An IOU splits one trade into two. When you buy, you only need the seller to have what you want. What they want next, they can spend the IOU on somewhere else.",
  "ch1.p4": "IOUs have a problem of their own: whoever holds one must believe the person who wrote it will make good. Set trust to 100%, and private IOUs work as well as bank IOUs. Set it to 10%, and for the first 30 rounds they are no better than barter. So the best IOU is one written by a third party everyone trusts. Today that third party is the bank, and behind the bank is the central bank. The number in your account is the bank's IOU to you: “I owe you this much. You can ask for it at any time, or pass it on to someone else.”",
  "ch1.take": "This model shows <strong>why</strong> IOUs work well, not <strong>how</strong> money actually appeared in history. The textbook story that barter came first and money later is disputed by anthropologists and historians, and many think record-keeping and credit came before coins. The model also leaves out forgery, default and the cost of storage.",
  "ch1.see": "Why exchange makes both sides better off, and what comparative advantage is: see Chapter 3 of Scarcity Moves (in Chinese) →",

  "ch2.title": "Loans create deposits out of nothing",
  "ch2.law": "When a bank makes a loan, it doesn't lend you someone else's deposit. It writes a new number into your account. That is how money gets created.",
  "ch2.p1": "The common account is that you deposit money at a bank, and the bank lends it to someone else. On that account, the bank is just a go-between, and the total amount of money never changes.",
  "ch2.p2": "What actually happens is this. You borrow 100,000 yuan from a bank to buy a car. The bank approves it and makes two entries in its books. On the asset side: “You owe me 100,000” (a loan). On the liability side: “I owe you 100,000” (the extra deposit in your account). No one's existing deposit has got smaller. A moment earlier, those 100,000 yuan of deposits didn't exist; a moment later, they do. The Bank of England puts it plainly in its public explanation: about 80% of the money in the UK is deposits created by commercial banks this way, notes and coins make up only about 3%, and most of the rest is the reserves commercial banks hold at the central bank.",
  "ch2.lab.title": "Looking at the whole banking system as one balance sheet",
  "ch2.lab.how": "Press “Make a loan” and watch both sides grow together; press “Receive a repayment” and watch money disappear. Switch between the two accounts, then press “Let banks lend until stopped” to see which limit bites first.",
  "ch2.cv": "Banking system balance sheet: reserves and loans on the left; deposits, central bank borrowing and capital on the right",
  "ch2.cv2": "Total deposits (the quantity of money) after each operation",
  "ch2.mode.label": "How the central bank supplies reserves",
