  "ch2.r.capv": "{v}% (required {k}%)",
  "ch2.r.resv": "{r} / {need}",
  "ch2.say.loan": "That loan just made assets (loans) and liabilities (deposits) <em>both</em> go up by 10. There are now 10 more yuan of deposits in the world, and nobody's deposit has gone down.",
  "ch2.say.repay": "When a borrower repays, the deposit in their account and their loan owed to the bank both fall by 10. Those 10 yuan of deposits <em>disappear</em>.",
  "ch2.say.qe": "The central bank buys bonds from households, and when it pays, banks' reserves and households' deposits both rise by 10. On the textbook multiplier, deposits can now reach up to <em>{max}</em>.",
  "ch2.say.stop.reserves": "Stopped by <em>reserves</em>: one more loan and required reserves would fall short. In the textbook account, deposits can be at most {mult} times reserves, which is {max}.",
  "ch2.say.stop.capital": "Stopped by <em>capital</em>: one more loan and capital would fall below {k}% of loans. Reserves are not the bottleneck. {borrow}",
  "ch2.say.borrowed": "The missing {b} of reserves was borrowed from the central bank at the policy rate.",
  "ch2.say.noborrow": "No reserves were borrowed from the central bank along the way.",
  "ch2.say.start": "Where things stand: the bank has just made a loan of 10 yuan. The loan on the left and the deposit on the right have both risen by the same amount.",
  "ch2.p3": "In the default textbook setup, the bank is stopped by reserves after just one more loan: the central bank supplies 10 yuan of reserves, the reserve requirement is 10%, and deposits can't go beyond 100. This is the textbook <strong>money multiplier</strong>: reserves times 1 ÷ reserve ratio gives the maximum deposits possible. On this account, the central bank controls the amount of money by controlling the <em>quantity</em> of reserves.",
  "ch2.p4": "Switch to “Today's central bank” and the same bank keeps lending for three more loans before stopping, and what stops it is capital, not reserves. Today's major central banks describe their approach this way: they set the <em>price</em> of reserves, the policy rate, and then supply whatever reserves banks need at that price. Reserves follow loans, rather than loans following reserves. What really limits lending are three things: whether there are creditworthy people who want to borrow, whether banks have enough capital to absorb bad loans, and whether the interest rate makes the business worth doing.",
  "ch2.p5": "The two accounts can be checked against reality. The Federal Reserve cut the reserve requirement to zero from March 26, 2020. On the textbook formula, the multiplier should have become infinite, yet deposits did not. From August 2008 to August 2014, the US monetary base (banknotes plus reserves) grew to about 4.8 times its former level, while M2 (mostly deposits) grew only to about 1.47 times. The People's Bank of China, by contrast, still actively uses the reserve requirement as a tool: after the cut in May 2025, economists estimate the average reserve requirement for financial institutions at about 6.2%.",
  "ch2.claim.h": "Where this is disputed, and how sure I am",
  "ch2.claim.1": "<span class=\"tag h\">Fairly confident</span> When banks make loans, they create deposits at the same time, and when loans are repaid, the deposits disappear. This is an accounting fact, and central banks' explanations agree on it.",
  "ch2.claim.2": "<span class=\"tag h\">Fairly confident</span> In systems like the US, UK and euro area, which operate through interest rates and have plenty of reserves, the quantity of reserves is not a hard limit on lending.",
  "ch2.claim.3": "<span class=\"tag m\">Even odds</span> Whether reserve requirements still bind elsewhere. In systems where reserves are tight and the central bank also adjusts reserve requirements (China, for example), they change banks' funding costs and the funds available to them. The effect is above zero, but how large it is depends on how the central bank also manages interest rates and open-market operations.",
  "ch2.take": "This model merges all banks into one, so it leaves out something: when you transfer a loan to a customer of another bank, your bank loses reserves. A single bank therefore cares about reserves more than the system as a whole does. The model also leaves out loan demand: in reality, “is anyone asking to borrow?” often matters more than “can banks lend?”",

  "ch3.title": "A bank with no losses can still fail",
  "ch3.law": "A bank turns money that depositors can withdraw at any time into loans that take years to come back. If enough people come for their money at once, even a bank that has lost nothing can fail.",
  "ch3.p1": "A mismatch is hidden in the balance sheet from the last chapter. Your deposit can be withdrawn at any time, while the mortgage the bank lent out takes thirty years to collect. Normally that's not a problem: few people come for their money each day, so the bank only needs to keep some cash on hand.",
  "ch3.p2": "The trouble is that every depositor knows this. If you hear that others are withdrawing, the most rational thing is to get there first, because those at the back of the line may get nothing. Nobody has done anything wrong; each person is protecting themselves, and the bank falls anyway.",
  "ch3.lab.title": "A hundred depositors, and a bank that has made no losses",
  "ch3.lab.how": "The bank keeps some cash and lends out the rest. A loan held to maturity pays back 1.5 times what was lent, but sold early it fetches only part of that. 20 people need their money anyway. Drag the size of the rumour and watch whether the rest follow. You can also change how much cash the bank keeps and what share of a loan's value a fire sale recovers, or switch on deposit insurance.",
  "ch3.cv": "A grid of one hundred depositors: who withdrew early and who got nothing",
  "ch3.cv2": "The more people withdraw early, the less each yuan is worth to those who stay until the end",
  "ch3.ctl.rho": "Scared into withdrawing by the rumour",
  "ch3.ctl.lam": "Cash the bank keeps",
  "ch3.ctl.s": "Fire-sale recovery",
  "ch3.out.rho": "{p}% ({n} people)",
  "ch3.out.pct": "{v}%",
  "ch3.out.s": "{pct}%",
  "ch3.btn.ins": "Deposit insurance",
  "ch3.btn.replay": "Run another day",
  "ch3.cv.x": "Depositors who withdrew early →",
  "ch3.cv.y": "↑ Paid per 1 yuan to those who stay",
  "ch3.cv.one": "1 yuan",
  "ch3.cv.thr": "Tipping point",
  "ch3.leg.need": "Need the money anyway",
  "ch3.leg.rumour": "Believe the rumour",
  "ch3.leg.follow": "Follow the crowd",
  "ch3.leg.stay": "Stay",
  "ch3.leg.unpaid": "Reach the front, nothing left",
  "ch3.r.w": "Depositors who withdrew early",
