  "ch3.r.unpaid": "Queued but got no money",
  "ch3.r.ins": "Insurer pays out",
  "ch3.r.people": "{v} people",
  "ch3.r.pct": "{v}%",
  "ch3.r.yuan": "{v} yuan",
  "ch3.r.none": "—",
  "ch3.say.calm": "Only {w} people withdraw early, still below the tipping point of {thr}%. Those who stay get back <em>{V} yuan</em> per 1 yuan at maturity, which beats withdrawing now, so no one follows the crowd.",
  "ch3.say.run": "The rumour sends {r} people to the bank. With those who needed their money anyway, {w0} are already queuing, close to the tipping point of {thr}%. The most nervous don't wait for the line to be crossed, <em>another {c} people follow</em>, withdrawals pass the tipping point, and staying no longer pays. Even by fire-selling its loans the bank raises only {paid} yuan, and the <em>{u} people at the back get nothing</em>. If nobody had panicked, everyone would have got back at least 1 yuan.",
  "ch3.say.runOver": "The rumour sends {r} people to the bank. With those who needed their money anyway, {w0} are queuing at once, already past the tipping point of {thr}%, so staying no longer pays and <em>another {c} people follow</em>. Even by fire-selling its loans the bank raises only {paid} yuan, and the <em>{u} people at the back get nothing</em>. If nobody had panicked, everyone would have got back at least 1 yuan.",
  "ch3.say.never": "When loans can be sold at full value, however many withdraw early, those who stay lose nothing. With no tipping point, the rumour can't spread.",
  "ch3.say.insured": "With deposit insurance, those who stay are sure of getting back at least 1 yuan, so they have no reason to follow the crowd. {w} people withdraw, the bank keeps running as normal, and the insurer <em>pays out nothing</em>.",
  "ch3.say.insuredPay": "The rumour brings {w} people in together. After the bank fire-sells loans to meet them, those who stay are left with only {V} yuan per 1 yuan, and the gap is filled by insurance: the insurer pays <em>{cost} yuan</em>. Depositors lose nothing, so no one has a reason to follow the crowd.",
  "ch3.say.insuredFail": "The rumour is so large that insurance can't stop it: {w} people come together, more than the bank can raise in cash. The insurer pays <em>{cost} yuan</em>, and depositors lose nothing.",
  "ch3.p3": "In the default setup the bank keeps 30% in cash and has no bad loans at all. If nobody panics, the 80 people who stay get back about 1.44 yuan for every yuan at maturity. But once more than 47.5% have withdrawn early, those who stay get back less than 1 yuan. Depositors don't all wait for that line to be crossed: in the model people differ in how nervous they are, and the most nervous go to the bank when withdrawals are still a dozen or so points short of it. So a rumour needs to send only 16 people who don't need their money yet; with the 20 who did, that is 36%, the most nervous can't sit still, and the rest follow one after another until everyone is at the counter. After the fire sale, only the first 65 in line are paid.",
  "ch3.p4": "This is a <strong>bank run</strong>, and the model economists cite most often comes from Diamond and Dybvig (1983). Its key point: the same bank can end up in either of two outcomes, one where everyone stays calm and one where everyone runs, and which one happens depends on what people <em>believe</em> others will do. Real examples are not far away. On March 9, 2023, customers withdrew about $42 billion from Silicon Valley Bank in a single day, and requests for about another $100 billion were queued for the next day; regulators closed the bank that next day. That run started not from a rumour but from the bank's real losses, yet it moved so fast because of the same mechanism.",
  "ch3.p5": "Try raising fire-sale recovery to 100%. The tipping point disappears: if the bank can turn its loans into cash at full value, a run is pointless. This is the role of the central bank as <strong>lender of last resort</strong>: it lends the bank cash against its good loans as collateral, so the bank doesn't have to sell them cheap. Deposit insurance takes another route: it promises those who stay that they will get their principal back, so nobody needs to rush. Switch insurance on and send the same rumour: the insurer pays nothing, because the run never starts. In the US, deposit insurance covers $250,000 per depositor, per bank, per ownership category. China has had deposit insurance since May 1, 2015; the maximum payout to one depositor at one bank is 500,000 yuan.",
  "ch3.take": "Limitations of the model: I set the depositors' panic level myself. In reality, how rumours spread and when regulators step in would change the outcome. The model also doesn't distinguish between banks that are really insolvent (liabilities exceed assets) and banks that just can't come up with cash right now (illiquid), and there is a large grey area between the two. Deposit insurance has its own cost too: when depositors stop caring what the bank does, the bank may take bigger risks.",

  "ch4.title": "Does more money mean higher prices?",
  "ch4.law": "Over the long run, money grows faster than goods, so each yuan buys less. But there is a moving variable between money and prices: how fast money changes hands.",
  "ch4.p1": "The first two chapters explained how money gets created. What happens if too much is created? The oldest answer is an identity: <strong>quantity of money × how many times each yuan changes hands in a year = prices × the amount of goods actually bought</strong>. The left side is the money spent in a year; the right side is the value, at prices, of the goods that same money buys. Both sides describe the same thing, so they are always equal.",
  "ch4.p2": "The identity itself doesn't say what drives what. To read “more money leads to higher prices” into it, you need two more assumptions: that velocity is roughly stable, and that real output is set by factories, workers and technology, not by how much money there is. The second roughly holds over the long run. The first depends: when money grows extremely fast, swings in velocity are small by comparison; when inflation is low, velocity often swings as much as money does (there is a US example later in this chapter). In the short run, both often fail. The model below puts the short run and the long run side by side: sellers set prices with inertia, first raising them by last year's inflation and then slowly catching up with actual demand.",
  "ch4.lab.title": "A year-by-year price machine",
  "ch4.lab.how": "For the first six years, money grows 2% a year, just keeping pace with output, and prices don't move. From year 1, money growth switches to the rate you set. Watch the gold line (money growth), the red line (inflation) and the teal line (output above or below normal). You can drag the sliders midway, or press “supply shock” to raise production costs by 8% at once.",
  "ch4.cv": "Money growth, inflation and output gap by year",
  "ch4.ctl.gM": "Money growth per year",
  "ch4.ctl.gV": "Change in velocity per year",
  "ch4.out.pct": "{v}%",
  "ch4.btn.shock": "Supply shock (costs up 8%)",
  "ch4.btn.reset": "Restart from year 1",
  "ch4.btn.finish": "Run all 30 years",
  "ch4.ax.x": "Year →",
  "ch4.ax.y": "↑ % per year",
  "ch4.leg.money": "Money growth",
  "ch4.leg.infl": "Inflation",
